PAX's copy of Cloudberry's regression suite on the port's cluster: the
differences from PAX's expected output that have been reviewed, and are the
port's for a reason of its own.

Each file is one test's difference in the form ../../singlenode/canon.pl
gives it, named for the expected output it differs from; a name with a pass
in it (test.orca.diff) holds for that pass alone.  ../../greenplum/run.sh
counts a test as passing when gpdiff.pl, under Cloudberry's init_file, PAX's
copy of it, the port's and this suite's, finds no difference, exactly a
file's here, or exactly one the greenplum suite keeps for the same test of
Cloudberry's own copy (../../greenplum/cloudberry): of greenplum_schedule's
tests PAX's copies mostly answer alike.

Each difference was read, and is kept for one or more of these reasons.  A
test whose difference is long, or not the same from run to run, is skipped
in the manifest instead, with what differs first.

PostgreSQL 19's own answers.  parallel_schedule is PostgreSQL 14's tests as
Cloudberry keeps them.  PostgreSQL 19 words its errors otherwise -- a
function or an operator no argument types match has a DETAIL and then the
HINT, where 14's had one HINT; "Key columns ... of the referencing table";
a subquery is "unnamed_subquery" -- and answers otherwise where it has done
more since: a record's NULL row populated (json, jsonb), an enum's new value
usable in its transaction, a cursor that cannot scan backward says so
(hash_index, xmlmap), an input it refused (date's five digits), pg_lsn in
eight digits, psql's \d, the constraints NOT NULL makes, the catalogs'
columns (type_sanity's attcacheoff), a leakproof function a superuser's
alone (create_function_sql); privileges' own -- the MAINTAIN privilege
("arwdDxtm"), no RULE privilege, a role's grants and "must be able to SET
ROLE", a security-restricted operation's safe search_path, which finds
none of the test's functions before the operation refuses them -- and
psql 19 passes over the data of a COPY FROM STDIN that failed, where 14's
read each line as a command ("invalid command \.", copy).

The port's names and settings.  A setting of Cloudberry's core that the
port has as a module's is shown by its name (resource_queue's SHOW
gp.resource_scheduler);
one it has not is unrecognized (gp_statistics_pullup_from_child_partition,
gp_allow_date_field_width_5digits, planner_work_mem, allow_segment_dml);
a table access method's handler is a function of its module's
(gp_ao.ao_column_handler); the port's extensions' tables and functions are
among a catalog's rows, and Cloudberry's own (gp_current_snapshot, the
complex type type_sanity's tab_core_types is made with, the test helpers
of regress_gp.c) are not; OIDs
the port allocates otherwise (oid_wraparound); TOAST tables PostgreSQL 19's
catalogs have not.

What the port does that Cloudberry's PAX refused.  A row fetched by its TID
(pax/src/access/pax_access_handle.cc): an AFTER INSERT row trigger fires
(triggers_gp, copydml; the port's own pax_copy_trigger runs copy2's, whose
UPDATE of the table its COPY fills runs on the segment), DELETE and UPDATE
... RETURNING return the old row
(returning_gp, fast_default), where Cloudberry's PAX said "not implemented
yet on pax relations: TupleFetchRowVersion"; and what follows from it, a
row the trigger's INSERT left there.  A PAX table's access method changed
by ALTER TABLE ... SET WITH, where Cloudberry's PAX refused to swap its
files with another method's (alter_distribution_policy).

What the port refuses otherwise than Cloudberry.  An exclusion constraint's
index is checked against the distribution once it is built, so PAX refuses
its gist index first (gpdist_opclasses); COPY FROM ... WHERE into a
distributed table; a subquery in ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE; an index built
CONCURRENTLY on a PAX table.

What PAX's expected output was made with.  The LIMIT query of
workfile/hashagg_spill, whose hash aggregate spills in Cloudberry's run and
not in the port's, which Cloudberry's own copy of the test ignores.

A finding, kept until it is fixed.  A role that has some of a table's
columns alone -- privileges' atest5, mtarget, t1 and errtst -- is refused
on the segments ("permission denied for table atest5") by an INSERT the
router sends, an upsert and a write the explicit write sends -- the
planner's route, which ORCA falls back to for an upsert -- which name
every column and are checked as the session's role there, where PAX's
expected output has PAX's own errors or the row's.  ORCA's own fragments
carry the coordinator's checks, and answer.
